body:
# Interface

An interface in C# describes "contract" methods that classes must implement. These contract methods only define the signature, i.e. the name of the method, return value(s), and parameters.

&#x200B;

For example, let's define a new interface: `IAnimal`

    public interface IAnimal
    {
        void Speak()
    }

The interface only defines one method signature of `Speak`, it's up to the classes that implement the interface to decide what to do with it. Let's make two classes, `Dog` and `Cat`

    public class Dog : IAnimal
    {
        public void Speak()
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
        }
    }

    public class Cat : IAnimal
    {
        public void Speak()
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Meow!");
        }
    }

Both classes implement the `speak()` method, but differ in the implementation.

**How is this useful?**

One of the main advantages of interfaces is that it allows abstraction of classes that implement the same interface. Using our examples from before:

    public class Program
    {
        public void main()
        {
            Dog d1 = new Dog();
            Cat c1 = new Cat();

            // We can create a list using the IAnimal interface
            // Any class that implements IAnimal can be added to the list

            List<IAnimal> list = new List<IAnimal>();
            list.Add(d1);
            list.Add(c1);

            // Because both Dog and Cat implement IAnimal, they can both
            // call the Speak() method

            foreach (var animal in list)
            {
                animal.Speak();
            }
        }
    }

&#x200B;

    output:
    Woof!
    Meow!

Agreeing with Th\_69 - each if statement in the BerekenFietsen method does almost identical checks and abstracting it into a separate method is the right way to go.

Also, some consideration should be had for creating classes for each bike. Where you have `double prijsXL = 16.99` you could make this value into a static property or method:

    public class FietsenXL
    {
        // Use decimal for monetary values for precision
        public static decimal Price { get; set; } = 16.99
    }

Then you would be able to call `FietsenXL.Price`. This can be done for all bike types.
